Release the fprintd device even after an aborted verify.

A device-level error during verify (fprintd emits verify-disconnected, e.g.
"Unexpected result from device 114" when the sensor is not ready yet right
after boot) sets m_abort. stop() then dropped the proxy without calling
Release, so fprintd kept the device claimed for the whole life of the shell
process. Every later Claim -- including pam_fprintd's from sudo, su and
polkit -- failed with "Device was already claimed", and those PAM stacks
silently fell through to the password prompt.

Release is best-effort already: releaseDevice() swallows sdbus errors, so a
genuinely disconnected device costs nothing beyond a failed call.

--- a/src/auth/fingerprint_authenticator.cpp
+++ b/src/auth/fingerprint_authenticator.cpp
@@ -116,11 +116,11 @@
   m_active = false;
   m_verifying = false;
   m_claiming = false;
-  if (!m_abort) {
-    releaseDevice();
-  } else {
-    m_device.reset();
-  }
+  // Always hand the device back; releaseDevice resets the proxy itself.
+  // Skipping Release on the abort path leaks the claim for the life of this
+  // process and locks every other fprintd consumer out of the sensor --
+  // pam_fprintd for sudo, su and polkit included.
+  releaseDevice();
 }
 
 bool FingerprintAuthenticator::createDeviceProxy() {
